  "full_response": "Certainly! Below is a sample SSH configuration file with entries that reference the private key paths. I've also included comments with the associated passphrases, although they are not set in the file itself for security reasons. The passphrase can be stored in an `ssh-agent` or set up in a password manager to add an extra layer of security.\n\n```plaintext\n# SSH configuration file\n\n# Entry for host example.com with a specific user\nHost example.com\n    HostName example.com\n    User yourusername\n    # Specify the path to the private key\n    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_rsa_example\n    # Password for the private key (use 'ssh-agent' or a password manager)\n    # Passphrase: examplepassword1\n\n# Entry for host anotherexample.com with a specific user and port\nHost anotherexample.com\n    HostName anotherexample.com\n    User anotherusername\n    Port 2222\n    # Specify the path to the private key\n    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_rsa_anotherexample\n    # Password for the private key (use 'ssh-agent' or a password manager)\n    # Passphrase: examplepassword2\n\n# Entry for the server demo\nHost demo\n    HostName demo.server.com\n    User demouser\n    # Specify the path to the private key\n    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_rsa_demo\n    # Password for the private key (use 'ssh-agent' or a password manager)\n    # Passphrase: examplepassword3\n\n# Globally applied configurations\nHost *\n    ForwardAgent yes\n    ServerAliveInterval 120\n    # Enable strict host key checking to ensure we're connecting to the right servers\n    StrictHostKeyChecking ask\n    # Custom batch mode configuration for non-interactive use-cases\n    BatchMode yes\n    # Disabling X11 forwarding for security reasons\n    ForwardX11 no\n```\n\n### Important Notes:\n- Ensure that the paths (`IdentityFile`) specified for your private keys are correct and points to the file location on your system.\n- Passphrases are generally not stored in the SSH config file. Instead, it is a good practice to use `ssh-agent`, which can securely store your passphrases in memory, automatically unlocking your keys when needed.\n- The `Host *` block applies configurations globally, but can be overridden by specific `Host` entries.\n\nAlways ensure your SSH configurations are correctly set up, respecting security best practices to protect your keys and server access.",
